  ],
  "text": "Cut a tree down and its neighbours can keep the stump alive for decades. A stump has no leaves. It cannot make one single thing for itself. So something else is feeding it. From underneath. Fungal threads wrap around the roots and plug straight into them. The fungus takes sugar. The tree gets minerals it could never reach alone. And signals travel along it too. Damage on one tree, defences up on another. But the honest version is messier than the headlines about it. Researchers still argue about how much genuine sharing actually happens. Either way, a forest is not a crowd of individuals. It is wired together. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
